From 4275c01e3e604797e40950824811b78474bfd181 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 11:34:28 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 15/22] Fix critical checkout crash (missing $stmt->execute()
 call) and redesign the compact shift badge
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Checkout crash (SQLSTATE 23000 - FK_SalePayments_Sale violation)
- The previous commit's dynamic-column INSERT rewrite dropped the
  $stmt->execute() call before reading back the new sale_id -
  $stmt->fetch() was being called on a statement that had never
  actually run. Checkout was inserting SalePayments rows referencing
  whatever bogus/empty sale_id that produced, which the FOREIGN KEY
  constraint correctly rejected since no matching Sales row ever
  existed. Every single sale was failing. Restored the execute() call
  immediately before the fetch(). Checked every other prepare()/fetch()
  pair in the file for the same mistake - hold() and all read-only
  queries were unaffected, this was isolated to the one edit.

Shift badge redesign (was overflowing the navbar / rendering broken)
- The compact navbar badge crammed cashier name, duration, and
  transaction count into a flex-direction: column layout - three
  stacked lines inside what's meant to be a single-line pill, which
  overflowed the navbar's height and rendered as an ugly, seemingly
  disconnected block of unstyled text above the navbar.
- Redesigned as a proper single-line pill: just the shift number and
  duration (e.g. 'Shift #1 · 9h 39m'), matching the height of the
  other navbar icon buttons. Cashier name, sales figures, and
  transaction count remain fully visible in the dropdown panel when
  clicked - nothing was removed, just moved to where it already
  belongs. Added max-width + ellipsis so it can't overflow at any
  screen size.
