From 65f78b23c701c1ced74baf6a2df0b0dfc0b2ecb5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <assistant@example.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 11:20:25 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 20/22] Fix Cashier (and any custom role) getting Access Denied
 despite having permission - migrate every page/controller from hard-coded
 role checks to the granular Roles & Permissions system

Root cause: this app has a full granular Roles & Permissions system
(Permissions/RolePermissions tables, a Roles & Permissions admin page,
SessionManager::hasPermission()/requirePermission() checking by
permission key) - but only pos.php/POSController.php had actually been
switched over to use it (in an earlier, uncommitted session). Every
other page and AJAX controller still used a hard-coded
requireRole(['Administrator', 'Manager']) (or just ['Administrator']),
which completely ignores whatever an admin configured in Roles &
Permissions. Granting a Cashier (or any other role) access to, say,
Sales History or Reports through the UI had no effect whatsoever -
those pages would deny them regardless, because the check never
consulted the permission grant at all.

Fixed by switching every remaining requireRole() call - across 14 page
entry points and 11 AJAX controllers - to requirePermission() with the
matching, already-seeded permission key (categories.manage,
customers.manage, customer_reports.view, inventory.manage, ledger.view,
products.manage, purchases.manage, reports.view, users.manage,
sales.view, settings.manage, suppliers.manage, activity_logs.view).
Administrator is unaffected either way (hasPermission() always returns
true for that role). Default access for Manager/Cashier is unchanged
from before this fix - only now it's actually configurable per the
Roles & Permissions page instead of hard-coded, which is the entire
point of that page existing.

Added the one missing piece: 'reconciliation.manage', since Transaction
Record & End of Day Reconciliation was built after the original
permission seed and never got a key of its own - new migration
(migration_reconciliation_permission.sql) plus updated pos_store.sql
and migration_default_role_permissions.sql (an earlier, also
uncommitted migration that backfills Cashier/Manager's default
permissions on an existing database, since a fresh Cashier role was
previously seeded with zero permissions at all) so Manager keeps its
existing Reconciliation access by default after this change, exactly
as it had it before under the old hard-coded check.
